## Microscopic and Mesoscopic Dual-Post-Synthetic Modifications of Metal-Organic Frameworks
## Introduction
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs） comprising various organic ligands and metal ions/clusters have been extensively investigated owing to their potential for gas storage, separation, sensing, and drug delivery.[1l Compared with traditional porous materials, such as zeolites，mesoporous silica, and activated carbon, the porous structures and properties of MOFs can be accurately controlled by the choice of building blocks. For the successful applications of MOFs, diverse functional groups have been introduced into pore surfaces. However, the direct use of functionalized organic linkers during MOF synthesis can restrict the synthesis conditions or result in the formation of MOFs with completely different structures. For this reason, post-synthetic exchanges, insertions, and modifications of MOFs have been widely studied.[2]  Since the desired functional  groups are introduced after MOF synthesis, a wider range of organic linkers, even those that can interfere with MOF synthesis， can be employed. In addition, in-depth studies on the resulting structureproperty relationship become possible if the functionalized structures can be crystallographically visualized.[3] However, a MOF often suffers from partial loss of crystallinity during postsynthetic modification (PSM). Moreover, the majority of organic reactions applied during PSM require additional reactive dangling groups.[4]  Since  structural  complexity  after  post-synthetic modification renders crystallographic visualization more difficult, ligands that are structurally simple but include strong directing groups should ideally be selected.
We herein report the dual PSM of a MOF involving the microscopic transformations of C-H bonds into C-C bonds and the mesoscopic introduction of hierarchical porosities (Scheme 1). Recently, our group demonstrated that $\mathsf{I}_{2}$ undergoes electrophilic aromatic substitution (EAS）with the electron-rich $m$ -DOBDC4- $\scriptstyle(m-\mathsf{D O B D C}^{4-}=\ 4,6$ -dioxo-1,3-benzenedicarboxylate） present in $\mathsf{C o}_{2}$ (m-DOBDC) through a single-crystal-to-single-crystal (SCSC) transformation.[5] Based on this finding, we speculated that analogous EAS reactions with electrophilic organic halides or carbonyl compounds at elevated temperatures would enable the introduction of stable C-C bonds by converting aryl C-H bonds (Figure 1a). Moreover, due to the higher electron density of C compared to that of H, it may be possible to crystallographically visualize this process. Post-synthetic modification through C-C bond formation has rarely been reported because of a lack of directing groups or suitable reaction conditions that maintain the MOF structure.[6] Notably, in the case of $m$ -DOBDC, the alkoxide $(\mathsf{R O}^{-})$ ，carboxylate, and C5 sites are reactive,while in the MOF structure, the former two sites are effectively blocked by metal-ion $(\mathsf{M}^{2+})$ coordination． Thus, $\mathsf{R O}^{-}$ becomes a strong ortho/paradirecting group during EAS. $\mathsf{M}_{2}(\mathsf{m}{\mathrm{-}}\mathsf{D}\mathsf{O}\mathsf{B}\mathsf{D}{\mathsf{C}})$ is isostructural with MOF-74 $(\mathsf{M}_{2}(p\mathrm{-}\mathsf{D O B D C});$ $\scriptstyle(p-\mathsf{D O B D C}^{4-}=$ 2,5-dioxo-1,4- benzenedicarboxylate), an iconic MOF with high $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ sorption capacity and catalytic activity.[7] However, owing to the low reactivity of the MOF-74 $p{\cdot}\mathsf{D O B D C}^{4-}$ moiety, the introduction of additional functional groups has been limited to open-metal-site coordination.[8] M2(m-DOBDC) can therefore be considered to be a cost-efficient, accessible, and structurally versatile MOF.
Intriguingly, because of the proximity of the introduced functional groups to the coordination bonds， steric hindrance lowers the stability of the coordination bonds， which facilitates the transformation into hierarchically porous meso-structures by etching with in situ generated protons (hydroniums) and halides. The resulting expanded polystyrene-like mosaic crystals contain hierarchical micro- and mesopores. Recently, the generation of hierarchical porosities has been suggested as a method of overcoming the limitations of microporous materials, such as slow mass transport and poor accessibility to large molecules,while maintaining their sorption capacities.[9] Alternatively, MOF-74 systems, with their longer linkers, mitigate transport limitations by providing large mesopores.[10] However, these systems have relatively low open-metal-site densities, which are generally the main adsorption sites for small molecules, such as ${\sf H}_{2}{\sf O}$ ${\sf H}_{2}$ p ${\sf N}_{2}$ ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ $\mathsf{O}_{2}$ , NO, ${\mathsf{N O}}_{2}$ $N H_{3}$ , l2, and hydrocarbons.[5,7a,] In addition, they only contain mesopores. Consequently, the adsorption and separation of small guest molecules in these systems are relativelyinefficient.Accordingly，themicroscopicand mesoscopic dual-PSMs of MOFs is expected to yield desirable properties while improving the diffusion rates of the guest molecules.[12]
## Results and Discussion
The eficiency of the reaction with $C H_{3}\vert$ prompted us to assess the reactivities of other organic iodides, namely $C_{2}H_{5}\vert$ and $C_{3}H_{7}\vert$ However， the substitution of $m$ -DOBDC with ethyl or propyl groups was slow due to the low reactivities of $C_{2}H_{5}l$ and $C_{3}H_{7}l$ Although the conversion ratios for $C H_{3}\vert$ and $C_{2}H_{5}l$ were $80\%$ after 24 and $48\mathsf{h}$ ， respectively, the high steric hindrance of the propyl group significantly lowered the conversion rate and conversion ratio for $|\mathsf{C}_{3}\mathsf{H}_{7}|$ (i.e., $74\%$ after $120{\mathsf{h}}{\mathsf{\cdot}}$ ）(Figure 1c). The crystal structures of the ethyl- and propyl-substituted $\mathsf{C o}_{2}(m{\mathrm{-}}\mathsf{D O B D C})\mathsf{s}$ were also resolved by SxRD, and the presence of the ethyl and propyl groups on the C5 atom of $m$ -DOBDC4- was confirmed, in addition to the coordination of I' to the open ${\mathsf{C o}}^{2+}$ sites (Figures 2c-2f).
The reactivity of $m$ -DOBDC was further explored using other halides, namely $C_{3}H_{7}B r$ and $C_{3}H_{7}C1$ SXRD was not possible due to the poor crystallinity following exposure to $C_{3}H_{7}B r$ and $\mathsf{C}_{3}\mathsf{H}_{7}\mathsf{C l}$ However, $^1\mathsf{H}$ NMR spectroscopy showed that the final conversion ratios were lower in both cases than that of $C_{3}H_{7}\vert$ ，although $C_{3}H_{7}B r$ initially reacted faster than $C_{3}H_{7}l$ (Figure 1c). This reverse reactivity implies that the smaller $C_{3}H_{7}B r$ diffuses rapidly into the pores. As expected, the least reactive $C_{3}H_{7}C1$ resulted in the slowest reaction.
$\mathsf{C o}_{2}$ (m-DOBDC) was then further subjected to EAS using carbonyl-based electrophiles.[14] Due to the low reactivities and steric hindrance of acetaldehyde and acetone (Figures S7 and S8), only formaldehyde reacted with $\mathsf{C o}_{2}(m\mathrm{-}\mathsf{D O B D C})$ at $60^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ .As indicated in Figure 1a, a hydroxyl group can be inserted onto the C5 atom of $m{\cdot}\mathsf{D O B D C^{4-}}$ without proton generation. Intriguingly, the previously introduced hydroxymethyl group was gradually converted into the methyl group， as confirmed by NMR observations (Figure S9). The coexistence of the hydroxymethyl and methyl groups was also confirmed by SXRD (Figures 2g and 2h), and the 40:60 ratio of these two groups observed by SXRD was consistent with that determined by NMR integration (45:55).
For simplicity, the formaldehyde-treated MOF is referred to as ${}^{"}{\mathsf{H C O H@C o_{2}(}}m{\mathsf{\_D O B D C}}{\mathsf{)"}}$ . Unlike the reactions with alkyl halides. the absence of a leaving group in formaldehyde resulted in the adsorption of water at the open $\mathsf{C o}^{2+}$ sites. In the proposed mechanism for the reduction of the hydroxymethyl to the methyl group,methanediol, the hydrated form of formaldehyde, acts as both a hydride donor and a proton donor (Scheme S1).[15] During the reaction, the hydroxymethyl group is protonated, and water is eliminated as the leaving group. The remaining stable benzylic carbocation intermediate is neutralized by the addition of hydride from the deprotonated methanediol. As a result, formic acid is generated as a by-product. Peaks attributed to formic acid appeared in the NMR spectrum after the direct decomposition of the MOF samples in $d_{6}$ -DMSO (Figure S10), implying that methanediol acted as a reducing agent.
Interestingly, when this substitution reaction was conducted at $100^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for $24\mathsf{h}$ , the MOF crystals retained their shapes during the acid-assisted decomposition of the MOF, while ${\mathsf{C o}}^{2+}$ was leached (Figure S11). Under the high temperature conditions, formaldehyde (or methanediol) is presumably polymerized into a resorcinol-formaldehyde resin.[16] In addition, no peaks due to H4m-DOBDC and H45-methyl- $m$ -DOBDC were observed in the NMR spectrum of the resulting polymer in $d_{6}$ -DMSO,indicating that $m{\cdot}\mathsf{D O B D C^{4-}}$ also participates in the polymerization reaction (Scheme S2). Thus, even when ${\mathsf{C o}}^{2+}$ is removed by acid treatment, highly porous organic replicas of the MOF crystals were obtained (Figure S12). The ${\sf N}_{2}$ uptake at $0.8~P/P_{0}$ and the Brunauer-Emmett-Teller (BET) surface area of the replica were determined to be $113.6~\mathsf{c m}^{3}/\mathsf{g}$ and $181.9m^{2}/9$ ， respectively (Figure S13).
