It is worth mentioning that reducing water affinity has been a long-term challenge in the context of MOF field applications, especially when unsaturated metal sites are present.[20] In particular, highly porous meso-structures are more vulnerable to hydrolytic damage. In our case, the nanosized ${\mathsf{C o}}_{2}(m{\mathrm{-}}{\mathsf{D O B D C}}).$ containing hierarchical micro- and mesopores showed higher water uptakes compared to the microporous $\mathsf{C o}_{2}(m\mathrm{-}\mathsf{D O B D C})$ (Figures 5 and S28-29).However, since the ${\mathsf{C o}}^{2+}$ open metal sites are the primary water-adsorption sites, the introduction of alkyl groups (even the shortest methyl group) at the C5 position can effectively reduce water adsorption. As a result, although the ${\sf N}_{2}$ uptake of $\mathsf{C o}_{2}$ (5-methyl- $.m.$ -DOBDC) at $0.03P/P_{0}$ was only $18\%$
Surface modification with formaldehyde also generated a rough surface along with a weak hysteresis in the ${\sf N}_{2}$ sorption isotherms, where formic acid generated during the reduction of the hydroxymethyl group presumably induced etching (Figure S31). However, the crystals were more intact than those obtained using the alkyl halides, since the co-existence of halides and protons after alkyl halide treatment further decreased coordination bond stability (Figure S32). Interestingly,when the MOF crystals were treated with formic acid solution, cracks appeared in the crystallographic ab planes of both ${\mathsf{C o}}_{2}(p{\mathsf{-D O B D C}})$ and $\mathsf{C o}_{2}(m-1)$ DOBDC) (Figure 6a). Layers of several tens of nanoparticles are connected by pillar structures to form highly porous mesostructures. In general, a reduction in the size of the MOF particle enhances the kinetics of sorption or catalytic reactions.[21] However, these small particles (especially nanoparticles) dispersed in solution require intensive purification and separation.
Thus, such meso-structures containing hierarchical micro- and macropores are beneficial for the practical applications of MOFs. As proof of concept, l2 adsorption was performed on the hierarchically porous MOFs and the second-order sorption rate constants were compared (Figure 6b and Table S2). Although the introduced methyl group prevented the chemisorption of ${\mathsf I}_{2},{\mathsf{I}}^{5,}$ 22] $\mathsf{C o}_{2}$ (5-methyl- $.m.$ -DOBDC) adsorbed $\mathsf{I}_{2}$ three times faster than the pristine MOF. In addition, the formic acid-treated $\mathsf{C o}_{2}(m{\mathrm{-}}\mathsf{D O B D C})$ and ${\mathsf{C o}}_{2}({p}{\mathrm{-}}{\mathsf{D O B D C}})$ exhibited almost three- and six-fold enhanced sorption kinetics, respectively, compared to the pristine MOF,which highlights the advantages of meso-structured MOFs.
## Conclusion
We successfully demonstrated the dual-PSM of a MOF through stable C-C bond formation while introducing hierarchical porosities. The SCSC transformation of Co2(m-DOBDC) during this modification enabled structural information to be unambiguously obtained. The resulting structures were confirmed to bear alkyl or hydroxymethyl groups on the C5 position of mDOBDC4- following EAS when alkyl halides or formaldehyde was used. In addition, the introduction of alkyl groups effectively prevented water adsorption onto the ${\mathsf{C o}}^{2+}$ open metal sites, even in the defect-enriched meso-structures. In contrast, the introduction of the hydroxymethyl group significantly enhanced water uptake at low pressures. Finally, we confirmed that the obtained hierarchically porous meso-structures exhibited significantly enhanced $\mathsf{I}_{2}$ sorption kinetics. These results confirm the ability of the dual-PSM protocol to introduce desired functional groups into MOFs while generating highly porous mesostructures.
